{
  "rationale": "Evidence from iteration 10 (score 690.0, the best so far): the oval finishes 20 of 20 at 2.14 s with rms 0.004. The s-bend finishes 20 of 20 at 2.47 s. The slalom never finishes (0 of 20). Its progress rose from 0.31 to 0.44 and line-loss events fell to about 1.05 per run. The kd sequence has helped at every step, but the gain is shrinking: kd 1.5 to 2.0 gave +139, and kd 2.0 to 2.6 gave only +16. Max error still sits at the 0.06 m off-track limit on the slalom with low rms, so the failure is overshoot and turn-in rather than jitter. Two effects are now probably limiting. Extra kd is giving diminishing returns, and at base_speed 1.2 the robot carries too much speed into the slalom direction changes. This is the final iteration, so I want a robust gain on the unseen track rather than a risky geometry change.\n\nChanges: (1) kd 2.6 to 2.8, a smaller step to keep damping the entries while limiting noise amplification (steering oscillation rose modestly from 0.29 to 0.29 on the oval, so there is headroom). (2) base_speed 1.2 to 1.1, to reduce centrifugal slide and line loss on sharp bends. Geometry, kp 5.0, ki 4.0 and last_direction line loss are unchanged, since they are the proven settings.\n\nExpected result: the slalom progress should rise above 0.44, with some chance of first finishes. The oval should still finish at about 2.3 s (about 2.14 times 1.2/1.1, plus a little from kd), and the s-bend at about 2.6 to 2.7 s, both under the 3 s limit. Score should stay near or above 690 if the speed cut is not too costly on completion time.\n\nHypotheses: (H1) slowing to 1.1 reduces slalom line loss and off-track exits, which would show as slalom progress above 0.5. (H2) If slalom progress does not change with speed, the limit is sensor edge loss on the direction changes, and the next step is a 7-sensor array at plus or minus 0.045 m. (H3) If the oval or s-bend completion time rises above 2.9 s, base_speed should return to 1.2.\n\nMeasurements requested per track: completion, progress, completion_time, rms_error, max_error, line_loss_events, steering_oscillation, control_effort, controller_errors and noise_robustness.",
